---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import Waterfall from '@/components/Waterfall.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import { exampleLanguages } from '@/lib/snippets'

const experimentLangs = exampleLanguages('experiment')
---

<Doc
  title="Regression testing"
  description="Turn a production run that went wrong into a permanent test case, run mcpd's built-in scorers over stored runs, and compare two versions of an agent with the sample sizes reported beside the deltas."
  lede="The run that broke on Tuesday becomes the case every later version has to pass."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>The loop this exists to close</h2>
  <p>
    An agent fails on a real question. Somebody reproduces it by hand, changes a
    prompt, and it looks better. Three weeks later it fails on the same question
    again, because nothing kept it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A dataset keeps it. Capture the failing run, and it stops being an anecdote
    and becomes a case with an input, an expected answer when you have one, and
    a pointer back to the trace it came from. Every later version runs against
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>Capturing a run</h2>
  <p>
    Find the run in the interface or through the API, then hand its trace id to
    the dataset.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="capture a failed run as a case"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/datasets/$DATASET/capture \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"trace_ids": ["b3d9e71a4c0f428eb6a5d2317f90c684"]}'`}
  />

  <p>
    The captured case keeps <code>source_trace_id</code>, so months later you can
    open the run that motivated it and read what actually happened rather than
    guessing from the input.
  </p>

  <Waterfall class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>The run's own answer is not the expected answer</h2>
  <p>
    <code>use_output_as_expected</code> is off by default, and that default is
    the important part. The runs worth capturing are usually the ones that went
    wrong, and recording a wrong answer as the reference would enshrine the bug:
    every later version would then be measured on its ability to reproduce it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn it on when you are capturing runs that were right, to hold a behaviour
    you already like in place. Leave it off, and write the expected answer
    yourself, when you are capturing a failure.
  </p>

  <h2>The built-in scorers</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd ships twenty-two deterministic scorers: ordinary code that reads a stored
    run and returns a number. They cost nothing, they never call a model, and
    they give the same answer twice, which is the property a regression test
    needs and a judge model does not have.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scorer</th><th>Passes when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>answered</code></td><td>The run produced a non-empty answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_errors</code></td><td>No span in the run failed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>exact_match</code></td><td>The answer equals the expected one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>contains</code></td><td>The answer contains a required string.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>json_valid</code></td><td>The answer parses as JSON.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>latency_budget</code></td><td>The run finished inside a millisecond budget.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cost_budget</code></td><td>The run cost no more than a set amount.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_tool_loop</code></td><td>No tool was called twice with the same input.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_efficiency</code></td><td>The run stayed inside a tool-call budget.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>not_a_refusal</code></td><td>The answer is not a refusal or an apology.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_leaked_secret</code></td><td>No key, token or credential appears in the answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>span_present</code></td><td>The run recorded a span of the required kind or name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_succeeded</code></td><td>The required tool ran, and at least one call of it ended ok.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>excludes</code></td><td>The answer contains none of the <code>forbidden</code> phrases.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>keyword_coverage</code></td><td>Graded: the share of <code>keywords</code> the answer mentions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>similarity</code></td><td>Graded: one minus the edit distance to <code>expected</code> over the longer length.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>matches_pattern</code></td><td>The answer matches the regular expression <code>pattern</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_call_accuracy</code></td><td>Graded: the share of <code>expected_tools</code> the run called.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_order</code></td><td>The run called <code>expected_tools</code> in that order, with other calls allowed between them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_not_called</code></td><td>The run called none of <code>forbidden_tools</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>answer_length</code></td><td>The answer has between <code>min_chars</code> and <code>max_chars</code> characters.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>json_schema</code></td><td>The answer is JSON, fenced or bare, that satisfies <code>json_schema</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>trajectory</code></td><td>The run&rsquo;s path: the tools it called against <code>expected_tools</code>, compared as <code>trajectory_match</code> says (<code>strict</code>, <code>ordered</code> or <code>unordered</code>, with partial credit), its steps against <code>max_steps</code>, and whether it reached an answer. The mean of the parts asked for. <code>GET /v1/api/traces/{'{id}'}/trajectory</code> shows the path it reads.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Text comparisons ignore case unless <code>case_sensitive</code> is set.
    Tool names are matched exactly against the names of tool-call spans of any
    of the four tool kinds. <code>json_schema</code> checks <code>type</code>,
    <code>properties</code>, <code>required</code>, <code>items</code>,
    <code>enum</code>, <code>minimum</code>, <code>maximum</code>,
    <code>minLength</code>, <code>maxLength</code> and
    <code>additionalProperties: false</code>, and names up to five violations in
    the reason. A pattern that does not compile, or a schema that is not an
    object, is refused with a 400 before any run is read.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/scorers</code> returns every scorer this daemon knows,
    with its <code>kind</code> (<code>code</code>, <code>judge</code> or
    <code>criterion</code>), its <code>direction</code>, the
    <code>config_keys</code> it reads, and whether it is
    <code>available</code>. A scorer that needs a setting and is not given one
    declines rather than guessing, and the decline names the setting.
  </p>

  <h2>When a model does the grading</h2>
  <p>
    Some questions no amount of deterministic code answers. Whether an answer
    actually addressed the question, whether a summary kept the meaning, whether
    a tone was right: those need a reader, and a model is the cheap reader.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judged scoring is off until you configure it, and a judged scorer never runs
    unless you name it. Both defaults exist for the same reason: grading spends
    money on every run it reads, and a feature that quietly does that is a
    feature you find out about on an invoice.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "judge": {
    "endpoint": "http://localhost:11434/v1",
    "model": "qwen2.5:14b",
    "api_key": "",
    "timeout_seconds": 30,
    "criteria": {
      "answer_relevance": "Does the answer address the question that was asked?",
      "no_hedging": "Does the answer commit, rather than hedge?"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The request is the OpenAI chat-completions shape, which is not a preference
    for a vendor: Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp, LM Studio, OpenRouter and the hosted
    providers all accept it, so the same configuration reaches a model on your
    laptop and a gateway in production. Leave <code>api_key</code> empty for a
    local model that wants none. The temperature is zero, because a judge that
    answers differently each time cannot be compared with itself, which is the
    only thing scores are for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Criteria are configured rather than passed on each request. A scorer id is
    stored on every score it produces and is what a later comparison groups by,
    so letting each call invent both the id and the question would build a
    column nobody can trust.
  </p>
  <p>
    Configured criteria appear in <code>GET /v1/api/scorers</code> with the
    kind <code>criterion</code>. A criterion with the id of a shipped rubric
    replaces that rubric on this daemon.
  </p>

  <h3>The shipped rubrics</h3>
  <p>
    With a <code>judge</code> block configured, nine rubrics are ready to name
    with no criteria written. Each runs in four steps. <strong>Preprocess</strong>,
    in code, gathers the question from the root span's input, the answer, the
    <code>expected</code> answer from the scorer configuration, and the context:
    every passage in the output of the run's <code>retrieval</code> and
    <code>rag_vector_operation</code> spans, or the <code>context</code> list in
    the configuration. <strong>Analyze</strong> is the one model call: the judge
    breaks the answer, the expected answer or the context into items and gives
    each a verdict of yes, no or unsure. <strong>Score</strong>, in code, counts
    the verdicts, so the number is arithmetic over itemised judgements and never
    a number the model chose. <strong>Reason</strong>, in code, names the items
    that cost the score.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rubric</th><th>Reads</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>faithfulness</code></td><td>answer, context</td><td>Share of the answer's claims the context supports.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>hallucination</code></td><td>answer, context</td><td>Share of the answer's claims the context contradicts. Lower is better.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>answer_relevance</code></td><td>question, answer</td><td>Share of the answer's statements that address the question.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>answer_correctness</code></td><td>question, answer, expected</td><td>Share of the expected answer's facts the answer conveys.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>context_precision</code></td><td>question, context</td><td>Average precision of the useful passages over their ranks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>context_recall</code></td><td>expected, context</td><td>Share of the expected answer's sentences the context covers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>context_relevance</code></td><td>question, context</td><td>Share of the passages that bear on the question.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>toxicity</code></td><td>answer</td><td>Share of the answer's statements that are toxic. Lower is better.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bias</code></td><td>answer</td><td>Share of the answer's opinions that are biased. Lower is better.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    An unsure verdict counts half. An answer with nothing to judge scores 0 on
    the three lower-is-better rubrics and declines on the others. Each score
    keeps the itemised verdicts under <code>metadata.steps</code>, the model,
    the attempts and the token counts under <code>metadata.judge</code>, and
    the cost of the call, priced with the daemon's price book, in
    <code>cost</code>. Its <code>scorer_version</code> names the rubric version
    and the model.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="run a judged scorer over the newest run"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/score \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"limit": 1, "scorers": ["answer_relevance"]}'`}
  />

  <p>
    <code>timeout_seconds</code> is how long one grading call may take, from
    connecting to the last byte of the reply: 30 by default, from 1 to 300. A
    call that runs past it is declined with the key named, and mcpd reads at
    most 1 MiB of a reply.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each judged scorer is one model call per run, and the request has to finish
    inside <code>--request-timeout-seconds</code>. So one request grades at most
    as many runs as fit in that deadline if every call takes the whole of
    <code>timeout_seconds</code>. With the defaults, 30 and 30, and one judged
    scorer, that is one run. A request for more, in <code>trace_ids</code> or
    <code>limit</code>, is refused with a 400 that names the cap, before any
    model is called. Leave <code>limit</code> out and the request grades as many of
    the newest runs as fit. Raise <code>--request-timeout-seconds</code> to grade more in one
    request. Each judged score is stored as soon as the model answers, so a
    request cut short keeps the scores it finished.
  </p>

  <h3>It declines rather than guesses</h3>
  <p>
    A judge that returns a number it did not derive is worse than no judge,
    because it looks like evidence. So every one of these is reported in
    <code>skipped</code> with its reason, and none of them produces a score.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What happened</th><th>What mcpd does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The run recorded no answer</td>
        <td>Declines. A run with no answer has not scored badly on relevance, it has not been measured.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model replied in prose</td>
        <td>Declines, quoting the reply. JSON wrapped in a fenced block or a sentence is still read.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model answered outside the scale</td>
        <td>Declines. A seven on a scale of one is not a nine tenths, and clamping it would invent a number.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model could not be reached</td>
        <td>Tries twice more, with a short pause, when the endpoint answered 429, 502, 503 or 504 or the connection failed, and only while the deadline leaves room. Then declines, with the status and the body. A rate limit is a reason, not a zero.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A rubric step could not finish</td>
        <td>Declines, naming the step: <code>preprocess</code> when the run lacks a piece the rubric reads, <code>analyze</code> when the reply is not a list of yes, no and unsure verdicts, <code>score</code> when there was nothing to count.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The reply was larger than 1 MiB</td>
        <td>Declines without reading the rest. A verdict is a few hundred bytes, so a reply that size is not one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The request deadline left no time for the call</td>
        <td>Declines without calling the model. The pass answers with the scores it finished, inside the deadline, rather than a 503.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every judged score records the model as its <code>scorer_version</code>.
    Two scores from two different judges are not comparable, and that field is
    what says so on the record.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every judged score also names the run of its own model call in
    <code>score_trace_id</code>. That run is one <code>scorer_run</code> span
    holding the prompt the judge was sent, the reply it wrote, its tokens and
    its cost, with a link to the run it scored. A score that looks wrong is
    opened and read like any other model call; in the app, the
    <em>judge call</em> link beside the score opens it.
  </p>

  <h2>Scorers you define</h2>
  <p>
    A project stores its own scorers with <code>POST /v1/api/scorer-definitions</code>.
    A <code>judge</code> definition is a criterion written for your agent, such
    as <q>The answer names the section of the refund policy it relies on</q>,
    graded from 0 to 1 by the configured judge. A <code>code</code> definition
    is one of the scorers above with its settings fixed, such as
    <code>keyword_coverage</code> with the keywords your answers must mention.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each save is a new version, and an identical body is not stored again. Name
    a definition wherever a scorer id is taken: <code>cites_policy</code> runs
    the latest version and <code>cites_policy@2</code> runs version 2. The
    version goes on every score, so changing a prompt never changes what an old
    score meant. A definition cannot take the id of a scorer mcpd ships or of a
    criterion in the config file. <code>GET /v1/api/scorers?project=</code>
    lists a project&rsquo;s definitions beside the shipped scorers, with kind
    <code>custom</code>. The Evaluations screen in the app creates and edits
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Live scoring</h2>
  <p>
    An evaluation rule scores runs as they finish, in the background, and
    writes scores with source <code>live</code>. It names the scorers to run,
    the share of runs to score, and which runs it is for: a root step name, a
    kind, an environment, an outcome, tags. Store one with
    <code>POST /v1/api/evaluation-rules</code>.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="score a tenth of checkout runs as they finish"
    command={`curl -X POST 'localhost:4317/v1/api/evaluation-rules?project=support-desk' \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"id": "checkout-quality", "enabled": true, "scorers": ["answered", "cites_policy"],
       "sample_rate": 0.1, "name": "checkout"}'`}
  />

  <p>
    A rule is stored off unless the request turns it on, and every scorer it
    names is resolved when it is stored. Which runs are sampled is decided by a
    hash of the rule id and the trace id, so the same run is in or out on every
    replica and after every restart, and two rules at 10% score different
    tenths. Each run is scored by a rule once. <code>max_judged_per_minute</code>,
    30 by default, caps the model calls a rule makes; a run over the cap is
    skipped, not queued. A change to a rule is a new version and applies from
    the next pass, every 15 seconds. Post the rule with
    <code>"enabled": false</code> to stop it.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring happens after the fact</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/api/score</code> runs scorers over runs that are already
    stored. Nothing evaluates inline. An evaluation in the request path adds its
    latency to the thing it is judging, and an evaluation that throws should
    never fail a user's request.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="score the last hundred runs"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/score \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"project": "support-desk", "limit": 100, "config": {"max_ms": 4000}}'`}
  />

  <p>
    The response carries the scores it wrote and, separately, every scorer that
    declined with the reason it declined. A scorer that could not answer is more
    interesting than one that returned a number, so mcpd never swallows it. The
    project's ingest ceiling reaches this door too: a score the pass computed
    that would take the project over <code>--quota-per-minute</code> is not
    stored, and it is listed in <code>skipped</code> saying so, rather than
    written past the ceiling or thrown away in silence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send <code>"dry_run": true</code> to see what a pass would produce without
    storing anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which runs a pass reads is decided by <code>trace_ids</code>,
    <code>from</code> and <code>limit</code>. Name the runs in
    <code>trace_ids</code> to score exactly those. Leave it empty and the pass
    takes the newest <code>limit</code> runs in the project, newest first, and
    <code>from</code> bounds that page: a pass sent <code>from</code> reads only
    runs that started at or after it, which is how you score everything since a
    deploy without listing ids.
  </p>

  <h2>Running an experiment from code</h2>
  <p>
    Every SDK has an experiment runner. Give it a dataset and your own
    function: it records an experiment, calls the function once per case inside
    a run that names the experiment and the case, scores each answer against
    that case's expected answer, and returns the results case by case with a
    mean per scorer.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    example="experiment"
    region="experiment"
    only={experimentLangs}
    title="run a dataset through your code"
  />

  <p>
    Four cases run at once unless you pass another <code>concurrency</code>.
    A case whose function raises is recorded as a failed run with its error,
    and the cases after it still run. Each run's root span carries
    <code>correlation.experiment_id</code> and the attribute
    <code>mcpd.dataset_item_id</code>, and spans your function opens nest under
    it, so a failed case opens in the waterfall like any other run. When the
    runner itself cannot finish, the experiment is marked <code>failed</code>
    with the reason. Read the results again later with
    <code>GET /v1/api/experiments/{'{id}'}/results</code>, and a run's path
    with <code>GET /v1/api/traces/{'{id}'}/trajectory</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing two versions</h2>
  <p>
    Record an experiment for each version you want to compare, tag its scores
    with that experiment's id, and ask mcpd for the difference.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="did the new prompt help"
    command="curl 'localhost:4317/v1/api/experiments/compare?baseline=v3&candidate=v4'"
  />

  <p>
    The answer is a per-scorer delta with the sample size on each side, a 95%
    confidence interval on the delta (<code>ci_low</code>,
    <code>ci_high</code>) and a two-sided <code>p_value</code>, both from
    Welch's t-test, which does not assume the two versions vary alike. Below
    twenty observations a side, <code>decidable</code> is false and nothing is
    called a regression.
  </p>
  <p>
    A scorer has <code>regressed</code> when it is decidable and the whole
    interval lies on the worse side of zero. Worse follows the scorer's
    <code>direction</code>: a fall in <code>faithfulness</code> is a regression
    and a fall in <code>toxicity</code> is an improvement. A mean that moved
    inside its own noise is reported with its interval and not as a finding.
  </p>

  <h3>Gating a deploy on it</h3>
  <p>
    <code>mcpd eval</code> asks a running daemon for the same comparison and
    turns it into an exit code: 0 when no scorer regressed, 1 when one did, 2
    when the comparison could not be made. Put it in the CI job that runs your
    candidate against the dataset, after the scores are stored.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="fail the build on a regression"
    command="mcpd eval --baseline v3 --candidate v4 --endpoint https://mcpd.internal"
  />

  <p>
    It reads <code>MCPD_ENDPOINT</code> and <code>MCPD_API_KEY</code> like
    the SDKs, prints a table of every scorer with its interval, or JSON with
    <code>--json</code>, and with <code>--require-decidable</code> also fails
    when a scorer has fewer than twenty observations a side.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this runs</h2>
  <p>
    All of it is the HTTP API, so the loop belongs in CI: capture failures as
    they happen, score the runs your test suite produces, register the build as
    an experiment, and fail the job when <code>regressed</code> comes back true
    on a decidable comparison.
  </p>

